From b98bd984a3d23838abd0a738f88dd47bf6632578 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 16:13:25 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 77/78] inline-3d(#103): broker the runtime connection from the
 browser process
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Branch B of the design of record, half one. Experiment E0 settled the open
question the whole plan hinged on: after LowerToken() the GPU process gets
ACCESS_DENIED opening the service pipe (its USER_LIMITED restricted token
matches neither ACE on the pipe's security descriptor — those serve a
USER_RESTRICTED profile and the WebXR AppContainer, not Viz), and its second
xrCreateInstance answered XR_ERROR_LIMIT_REACHED, which makes destroy-then-create
mandatory rather than merely tidy. So the browser process brokers.

WHAT ONE LADDER RUNG IS

  1. Recover THIS process's own snap/query session — ResetForRetry +
     InitializeCore, 0071's pair, unchanged. That single xrCreateInstance does
     three jobs at once: it is the liveness PROBE, it is what RELAUNCHES
     displayxr-service (nothing else on the machine does — there is no watchdog,
     and a sandboxed GPU process cannot spawn children), and it is what restores
     the rig locates every inline-3D SCENE reads.
  2. Export a fresh, connected, UN-HANDSHAKEN endpoint from the recovered
     instance (xrWeaveExportIpcConnectionDXR, runtime RC-4 / PR #1108).
  3. Answer the GPU's RequestWeaveReconnect with it. Mojo duplicates the HANDLE
     into the target itself; there is no OpenProcess/DuplicateHandle anywhere in
     this path, and no standing capability is granted — one connection, chosen
     by the browser, at a moment the browser chose.

The export deliberately does NOT handshake: connection setup is connect ->
shm transfer -> version check -> client description, and the last three MUST run
in the ADOPTING process or the service duplicates shared memory into the wrong
address space and identifies the wrong peer.

ONE LADDER, ONE STATE WORD

0071's degraded-rig re-negotiation and this disconnect are orthogonal axes of the
same machine, so they share RenegotiateDelaySeconds (2/5/15/60/300s) rather than
growing a second backoff — now also exposed as ReconnectDelaySeconds() because
the GPU side needs the same numbers. A DISCONNECT SUPERSEDES A DEGRADE: you
cannot re-negotiate the rig on a dead pipe, so the rig ladder stays suppressed
for the whole outage and is re-armed on the way back up if InitializeCore still
finds the rig refused.

|renegotiable| keeps its exact meaning — "this process can create an OpenXR
instance on its own" — and is therefore the discriminator that makes ArmReconnect
Ladder a no-op in the GPU process, whose ladder is the broker one.

DETECTION, BROWSER-SIDE

xrLocateViews now routes through the #103 classifier. It is a CORE entry point,
so unlike the four weave ones it has always gone through OXR_CHECK_XRET: a dead
pipe surfaces as XR_ERROR_INSTANCE_LOST on the first call, no streak needed. That
matters because in sync mode it is the only runtime call this process makes, and
without it the browser learns nothing until the GPU's mojo report arrives — which
never comes if the GPU is idle.

VERSION SKEW (runtime #1094)

InitOutcome gains kVersionSkew, from XR_ERROR_RUNTIME_VERSION_SKEW_DXR. One
loud, unthrottled, once-per-process line naming the remedy ("relaunch DisplayXR
Browser"), then the 5-minute tail — never permanent, because an installer that
finishes writing or a rollback recovers without a relaunch. The two git tags are
in the runtime's own client log; the OpenXR surface does not expose them, so the
line points there rather than inventing them. A cold-start skew skips the
500ms retry chain entirely: 120 fast retries cannot change a build mismatch and
would only bury the one line that says what to do.

THE COLD-PROFILE isSupported RACE (observed while running E0)

A cold profile's first page load probed isSessionSupported('inline-3d') ~4s
BEFORE xrWeaveBindWindowDXR completed, was told no, activated nothing, and never
re-asked; a reload wove instantly. Same family as this issue — support state
answered before it is knowable — and it has to be fixed by DEFERRING rather than
re-announcing: nothing in the renderer-facing surface can push a correction to a
page already told no, and building one would mean changing blink mojom.
IsInline3DSupported now holds the reply until the answer exists (the session
binds) or a 5s deadline, whichever is first, and answers synchronously the
moment init has finished either way.

NEGATIVE CONTROL

Unchanged and re-checked at every new entry point: with no DisplayXR runtime,
InitOutcome::kUnavailable latches within the first seconds, the sync-session
latch stays false, BrokerIpcConnection refuses before creating anything (that
guard is the load-bearing one — it is driven by IPC, not by our own state), every
later support probe answers false synchronously, and no timer is ever armed.

HANDLE HYGIENE (design §6.8)

Every attempt is owned end to end. The exported endpoint is wrapped in a
ScopedHandle the instant it crosses into C++, so it is closed on every path that
does not transfer it. A flapping service must not leak one pipe INSTANCE per
attempt: the service's instance count is finite and exhausting it would deny
service to every DisplayXR client on the box, not just this browser.

Files: common/displayxr_weave.mojom (ReconnectStatus, ReconnectResult,
RequestWeaveReconnect), browser/displayxr_weave_client.{h,cc},
browser/displayxr_weaver_impl.{h,cc}, browser/displayxr_service_impl.cc.
Zero upstream-Chromium files.
